<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wu (state)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">to 473 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wu was a state of the Zhou period in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> region, centred on modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>, whose rulers claimed descent from Taibo, an uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou">King Wen of Zhou</a>. It rose to power in the late sixth century BCE under King Helü, who defeated Chu at Boju in 506 BCE, and under his son Fuchai, who challenged Jin for leadership of the states. It was conquered by its southern neighbour Yue under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goujian">Goujian</a> in 473 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wu-state.jpg" alt="Wu (state)" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yug · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese_plain_5c._BC-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Wu was a state of the Western Zhou and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spring-and-autumn-period">Spring and Autumn period</a> that lay outside the Zhou cultural sphere. It was also known as Gouwu or Gongwu, from the pronunciation of the local language. It stood at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, east of Chu and south of Qi, and its territory reached north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuzhou">Xuzhou</a> and Lianyungang, west to the Dabie Mountains and south to Lake Tai. Its first capital was Meili, in modern Wuxi, then Helü's City, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Gusu</a>, probably modern Suzhou. Sima Qian recorded a founding myth: Taibo, eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gugong-danfu">Gugong Danfu</a>, and his brother Zhongyong left Zhou so that their youngest brother Jili, father of King Wen, could inherit, and settled in Wu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Wu rose in the 6th century BCE with the help of Jin, which found it a useful ally against Chu. In 584 BCE it rebelled against Chu on the advice of Wuchen, a Jin minister who had defected from Chu. Wu Zixu, whose father and brother King Ping of Chu had murdered, fled to Wu and helped Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a> assassinate King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and take the throne as King Helü. In 506 BCE Helü led the army against Chu with his brother Fugai, Wu Zixu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-tzu">Sun Tzu</a>, and defeated a strong Chu army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boju">Battle of Boju</a>. King Zhao of Chu fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and the Wu army captured the Chu capital, Ying. Wu defeated Qi in 484 BCE.</p><p>Chu then aided the rise of Yue, Wu's southern neighbour. Wu captured the Yue king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goujian">Goujian</a>, but King Fuchai of Wu let him live as his servant and then return home. While Wu campaigned in the north, Goujian attacked in 482 BCE and took the capital. Wu could not recover, and Yue absorbed the state in 473 BCE. Fuchai's sons were exiled, and their descendants took Wu as their clan name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Wu is generally regarded as having developed the first Chinese navy, with classes of ships described in a dialogue between Helü and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zixu">Wu Zixu</a> preserved in the Yuejueshu. Wu and Yue were masters of metallurgy, and their swords, with a high proportion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, bore inscriptions and inlay. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> wrote that the people of Wu wore their hair short and tattooed their bodies, and its language differed from the Old Chinese of the central states. Wu is still a name for the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and for Wu Chinese, and was used by Eastern Wu of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a> and by Wu and Wuyue of the Ten Kingdoms.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wu (state)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 506 BCE the <b>State of Wu</b>, advised by the Chu exile Wu Zixu, defeated Chu at Boju and occupied Ying; King Zhao fled, and the state survived only with help from the State of Qin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Military</a></li></ul></section>
